**Proposition 10.9 (Native pure reconstruction is not mixed tomography).**
Let

$$
\rho_0=\frac1{10}I_5+\frac12|+\rangle\langle+|,
\qquad |+\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt5}(1,1,1,1,1)^{\mathsf T},
$$

and

$$
\rho_\pm=\rho_0\pm\frac1{50}(|1\rangle\langle3|+|3\rangle\langle1|).
\tag{10.17}
$$

Both are positive definite because $\lambda_{\min}(\rho_0)=1/10$ and the
perturbation norm is $1/50$. They have the same diagonal $p_i=1/5$ and the
same five native coherences $C_{ij}=1/10$, but their $1$--$3$ entries differ.
Thus the pure full-support, calibrated-phase hypotheses are essential.

The native formulas give exact expectations in the declared source model;
they are not finite experimental acquisition claims. The reconstruction is
neither mixed-state tomography nor full Yoneda data. In particular, a mixed
state is not assumed to obey the pure-state nonlinear transport law.
Proof. The eigenvalue and norm bounds give positivity by Weyl's inequality.
The perturbation changes only the unmeasured $1$--$3$ pair, so the stated
native data agree while the density matrices are distinct. $\square$

